1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:03,797
This rock has 245 animals living inside it.

2
00:00:04,283 --> 00:00:07,319
It's a metal nodule from the deep Pacific seafloor.

3
00:00:07,319 --> 00:00:10,891
These rocks grow just a few millimeters every million years.

4
00:00:11,383 --> 00:00:17,849
This month, scientists reported cracking open 244 nodules.

5
00:00:17,849 --> 00:00:20,301
Inside, 4,139 animals.

6
00:00:20,817 --> 00:00:23,227
The average nodule held about seventeen.

7
00:00:23,227 --> 00:00:25,759
Two hundred and forty-five was the record.

8
00:00:26,059 --> 00:00:28,478
Like and subscribe for more ocean science.

9
00:00:28,983 --> 00:00:33,472
Tiny roundworms and crustaceans, under a millimeter long, live in the cracks.

10
00:00:33,472 --> 00:00:36,737
And they differ from the animals in the mud around them.

11
00:00:37,250 --> 00:00:37,928
The catch:

12
00:00:37,928 --> 00:00:42,066
nodules hold nickel, cobalt and copper, and miners want them.

13
00:00:42,066 --> 00:00:47,695
Co-author Sabine Gollner warns mining may mean an irreversible loss of unique life.

14
00:00:48,183 --> 00:00:50,850
Now, what if every volcano erupted at once?

15
00:00:50,850 --> 00:00:52,462
Full story on the channel.
